1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/32203783/iver-haugen
2 CONT
2 CONT This is an account, as clearly as remembered, told to me, Laureen Mikkelson Hayes, by my Grandfather, Iver Haugen, on Tuesday, July 27, 1976. It is his story of his family's arrival in this country from Norway in the year 1894.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family arrived by ship across beautiful, quiet waters at Ellis Island in New York. Grandpa remembers seeing the Statue of Liberty. He also remembers the check for small pox vaccinations as they were leaving the boat. It seems 
2 CONC his mother's vaccination had never taken, but she had an old scar from years before when her brother had accidently hit with a knife while whittling. so in the quick glance check for vaccination scars, when everyone rolled up thei
2 CONC r sleeves and filed by men on the way off the boat, Great Grandmother Haugen's knife-wound scar was mistaken for a small pox vaccine scar and she was passed with everyone else.
2 CONT
2 CONT After leaving the boat they go on a train heading for North Dakota, where Great Grandfather's brother John lived. Everyone got on the same train except for Joe, who got a different-colored ticket and was put on a train going throu
2 CONC gh Canada. Great Grandmother was very upset about this separation and felt they would never meet again in such a huge, unknown country. After changing trains in St Paul, MN, the family was very surprised when the train stopped in 
2 CONC Minneapolis, just acress a river and stayed there for what seemed like a long time. Then, lo and behold, another train pulled up next to them and Great Grandmother said, "Why I do believe I see Joe!" Sure enought, they had been wa
2 CONC iting in Minneapolis to connect with the train Joe was riding.
2 CONT
2 CONT Having been reunited, the family proceeded to Sanborn, North Dakota, where the Haugens left the train to wait for a one-car shuttle train that went to Hannaford. While waiting, Great Grandfather walked over to a church across from
2 CONC  the depot. It was open, so he walked inside. In a few minutes, the rest of the family joined him. Iver says that he will never forget his father's words as they stood in the obviously abandoned church: "They sure don't take good 
2 CONC care of their churches here in America!"
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon the car for Hannaford arrived and they all boarded. After a short time, the car stopped at a platform with a small telegraph office at one end. They were told that this was Hannaford. Great Grandfather said that he thought Ha
2 CONC nnaford was a town! Instead, they found a hotel, a butcher shop, and a blacksmith, in addition to the telegraph office. While standing on the platform, the family was approached by a young lady, who, to their surprise, spoke perfe
2 CONC ct Norwegian. She asked if they were the Haugens. If so, John, Great Grandfather's brother, should be coming to meet them soon, as he had been meeting the train the past few days hoping to find that his brother's family had arrive
2 CONC d from Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly, a one-armed man rode into "town," tied his horse in front of the hotel and Great Granfather walked over to talk to him as them had been friends in Norway. The one-armed man said that he knew where John lived and would giv
2 CONC e them directions. So the family started walking out of town towards the east, then toward the south. They walked, and walked, and walked, and walked. . . . Finally, they came to a large flat rock, at which time Great Grandmother 
2 CONC announced that she was going to rest. So the family put down the baggage that they had been carrying and rested.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the short rest, everyone gathered his/her bags and started walking again. Happily, they soon saw a wagon coming toward them. Grandpa Iver thought it to be a really fancy model as it had springs under the seat, which gave a "
2 CONC smooth" ride. On the wagon were three people: Grandpa Iver's uncle John, John's wife, and a baby in the wife's arms. The baby was Bennie H Haugen, the first cousin Grandpa ever met in America.
2 CONT
2 CONT Having met, the family got into the wagon and Uncle John drove them to his home. There were two houses on his land, a wooden fram house John had just finished building for his wife and son, and a sod house, which had been the John
2 CONC  Haugen's first home. It was in this sod house that Iver and his family lived while they began breaking ground for their own homestead.
2 CONT
2 CONT That fall 10 acres was broken with the aid of two white oxen and a plow. While plowing, one needed to carry tools for pounding dents out of the blade, which resulted from hitting rocks. The summer of 1895, the first crop was plant
2 CONC ed and resulted in a great crop. Next summer, twenty acres was to be broken and since Great Grandfather had a great deal of difficulty doing the breaking, planting, etc by himself, Iver was given the task of breaking 10 acres by h
2 CONC imself. It was a rough summer with a poor yield, but it was also the year they contracted to buy the land from a Mr Landon from NY.
2 CONT
2 CONT This was the beginning of life for Grandpa Iver in America. Memories which he shared with me this summer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Laureen G Hayes September 14, 1976
2 CONT
2 CONT *************8
2 CONT
2 CONT IVER was born in 1883.  He married Astrid Olson from Norway in 1908.  A child, Anna Amalie was born in 1909 and died the same year.  The mother died of pneumonia in 1910.  Iver left for Petersburg, Florida returning to marry Gunhi
2 CONC ld Fyllingslid from Stamnes, Norway in 1915.  They lived a while at St. Petersburg, Florida raising poultry then moved to Fargo and back to farming in Greenfield Township.  Their children are:
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene (Mrs. Lars Mikkelson) - 1915-1958 John - 1917-1954 Carl J. L. - 1918, Walum Gerda (Mrs. Eldren Haugen) - 1920, Hannaford Howard.  - 1921, Fargo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Iver was a member of the old Union brass band, played clarinet.  He also belonged to a band at Petersburg, Fla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gunhild died in 1956.  Iver is now 92 years old and has lived with his daughter, Gerda, and family since 1956.
2 CONT
2 CONT Naturalization record: Haugen, Ivar   Norway   May 19, 1921   2nd   Griggs   P-14   072
